---
import ExternalLink from '../components/ExternalLink.astro';
import LegalPage from '../components/LegalPage.astro';
import { contact, legal } from '../config';
---

<LegalPage
  title="Privacy notice"
  kicker="Privacy"
  description="How PlayRun handles the details you send through the contact form, the anonymous visit statistics, what the site stores in your browser, and your rights."
  lead="This site collects very little. Here is exactly what, why, where it goes, and how long I keep it."
  updated="2026-11-02"
>
  <section class="row">
    <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
    <p>
      The controller is {legal.controller}{legal.address ? `, ${legal.address}` : `, ${contact.location}`}. Email{' '}
      <a href={`mailto:${contact.email}`} data-umami-event="get-in-touch" data-umami-event-where="legal-email">{contact.email}</a> for anything in this notice.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="row">
    <h2>The contact form</h2>
    <p>
      When you send a message I receive your name, your email address, the message itself, the address of the page
      you sent it from, the time you opened that page and the time the message arrived. I use these only to reply to
      you, and the two times only to keep out automated spam.
    </p>
    <p>The lawful basis is my legitimate interest in answering people who write to me.</p>
    <p>
      The form delivers the message into my Google Workspace account: Gmail, a Google Sheet and the Apps Script that
      connects them. Google processes that data on my behalf under its Workspace terms.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="row">
    <h2>Hosting</h2>
    <p>
      The site is hosted on GitHub Pages, behind Cloudflare. Cloudflare processes each request, including your IP
      address, to deliver the site and protect it, under its own{' '}
      <ExternalLink href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">privacy policy</ExternalLink>. GitHub may log the IP
      address of each request for security, under its own privacy statement. The fonts and every script are served from
      this site. Beyond those two, only two services receive anything: the contact form, which is sent to my Google
      Workspace, and the visit statistics below, which are sent to Umami. There are no cookies.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="row">
    <h2>Visit statistics</h2>
    <p>
      I use Umami for anonymous, cookieless visit statistics. It sets no cookies and stores nothing in your browser.
      For each page you view it receives the page's address and title, the site you came from, your browser, operating
      system and type of device, your screen size and language, and your country, which Umami works out from your IP
      address without keeping the address.
    </p>
    <p>
      It also counts a few clicks, with no personal details and nothing you type: Get in touch and email links,
      LinkedIn links, a game's store link (with the game's name), See every game I've tested, a message sent through
      the form or the email way round being offered, starting and finishing Bug Sweep, running the Quality steps and
      the device you pick there, tracking or ignoring the glitch, unlocking an achievement, and the Motion switch.
    </p>
    <p>
      None of this identifies you. If your browser sends Do Not Track, nothing is sent at all. The lawful basis is my
      legitimate interest in knowing which pages people read. Umami processes the statistics on my behalf under its{' '}
      <ExternalLink href="https://umami.is/privacy">privacy policy</ExternalLink>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="row">
    <h2>What the site stores in your browser</h2>
    <p>
      None of this is a cookie, and none of it ever leaves your browser or is sent to me. It is there only so the site
      remembers your choices. In your browser's local storage, kept between visits:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>the games: the bugs you have caught, the achievements you have unlocked, and whether the games are on or off;</li>
      <li>your Motion choice, on or off, once you have used the switch in the header;</li>
      <li>whether you have tracked the glitch on the Home page, so it stops appearing;</li>
      <li>the browser or phone you picked on the Quality page, so its steps show next time.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      In session storage, which your browser clears when you close the tab: whether the TAP hint for the games has been
      shown on this visit.
    </p>
    <p>
      Reset on the scoreboard clears the bugs you have caught, your achievements and the glitch. It keeps whether the
      games are on or off, your Motion choice and your Quality device choice: change those with their own switches, or
      clear this site's data in your browser's settings to remove everything.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="row">
    <h2>How long I keep it</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Messages in my records are deleted after 24 months.</li>
      <li>Visit statistics stay in my Umami account for as long as its plan keeps them. They hold nothing that identifies you.</li>
      <li>Nothing else is collected, so there is nothing else to keep.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="row">
    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      You can ask for a copy of the personal data I hold about you, ask me to correct or delete it, and object to how I
      use it. Email <a href={`mailto:${contact.email}`} data-umami-event="get-in-touch" data-umami-event-where="legal-email">{contact.email}</a> and I will answer. If you are not happy with
      my answer you can complain to the Information Commissioner's Office at{' '}
      <ExternalLink href="https://ico.org.uk/">ico.org.uk</ExternalLink>.
    </p>
  </section>
</LegalPage>
